            <td data-label="Why it matters">{row.why}</td>
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>
    This is where a <strong>high-income skill portfolio</strong> approach beats chasing a single credential.
    A PR degree opens one door. It does not by itself build the media relationships, the crisis judgment,
    or the digital and measurement fluency that actually move you up the pay ladder. The professionals who
    progress fastest stack the degree with real writing samples, documented coverage wins, and comfort
    with both traditional and digital channels, rather than betting everything on the degree alone doing
    the work.
  </p>
  <div class="highlight-box highlight-box--teal">
    <p>
      A portfolio of three real pitches and one documented piece of earned coverage beats a transcript
      full of A grades in every PR interview that actually matters.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="salary-by-experience">What PR professionals actually earn</h2>
  <p>
    Pay in Indian PR climbs slowly at the start and then accelerates once you own client relationships
    directly. Here is the realistic range by stage, based on current market data:
  </p>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Career stage</th>
          <th>Typical annual pay</th>
          <th>Context</th>
