  <h2 id="digital-shift">How digital and influencer PR changed the job</h2>
  <p>
    PR in India today is not the same job it was a decade ago, and the industry's own data confirms it.
    According to PRCAI's 2026 SPRINT industry report, India's PR market grew 11% to roughly Rs 3,230
    crore in the 2025-26 financial year, and is on track to reach about Rs 4,500 crore by 2030. Inside
    that growth, the client mix has shifted sharply: government clients now make up around 11% of the
    top client categories, up from about 4% just a few years ago, and start-ups have expanded their share
    from roughly 6% to 22% over the same period, while private corporate share has slipped slightly.
  </p>
  <p>
    Two other shifts matter directly for anyone starting a PR career now. First, regional PR (work outside
    the traditional metro-only focus) has grown from about 10% of industry revenue three years ago to
    roughly 19% today, and is projected to reach a quarter of the industry — meaning tier-2 and tier-3
    market experience is becoming a real career asset, not a consolation prize. Second, more than 40% of
    communicators now say CEOs are actively seeking external strategic counsel from their PR advisors,
    which means the profession is being asked to do more strategic thinking, not just media outreach.
  </p>
  <p>
    On the ground, this shows up as briefs that routinely include an influencer or creator layer alongside
    traditional media, "digital PR" work aimed at earning online mentions and links rather than only print
    or broadcast coverage, and social listening tools used to track sentiment in near real time instead of
    a monthly clipping report. A PR professional who only knows how to pitch a newspaper reporter is
    genuinely under-equipped for a modern brief.
  </p>
  <div class="honest-take">
    <p class="honest-take__label">Honest take</p>
    <p>
      Do not treat "digital PR" and "traditional PR" as two separate career tracks you choose between.
      Almost every real brief today blends both, and the professionals who can move fluidly between a
      print journalist pitch and an influencer coordination brief are the ones clients keep renewing
      contracts with.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="ai-impact">What AI is actually doing to PR work</h2>
  <p>
    This is not a future risk — it is already showing up in the numbers. PRCAI's 2026 report found that
    AI spending by Indian communication teams has risen from roughly 2% to 7% of revenue, with content
    creation and research as the top applications. AI tools can now draft a first-pass press release,
    build a media list, and summarise coverage in minutes — tasks that used to take a junior executive
    hours.
  </p>
  <p>
    The more useful framing is not "will AI replace PR professionals" but "what changes about what a PR
    professional actually does." A junior executive today can supervise AI tooling to produce drafts and
    research that once took two or three people working manually, which raises the bar for what a junior
    hire is expected to add on top: judgment, accuracy checking, and relationship-building that AI cannot
    fake. One notable warning sign from the same report: fake-news incidents reported by corporate
    communicators nearly doubled, from 28% to 46% year over year, and roughly 80% of communicators now
    flag AI-generated misinformation and deepfakes as a top reputational risk — meaning fact-checking and
    verification skill is becoming a bigger part of the PR job, not a smaller one.
  </p>
  <div class="honest-take">
    <p class="honest-take__label">Honest take</p>
    <p>
      This makes the entry bar higher, not lower. Fewer routine drafting hours are needed per junior
      hire, so the juniors who get kept and promoted are the ones who can direct AI tools competently and
      catch a wrong fact or a wrong tone before it goes out under a client's name. Build that fluency
      before your first interview, not after you get the offer.
    </p>
  </div>

